  "titleEn": "Placement test",
  "instructions": "This test finds where you should start. It goes up one level at a time and stops once questions get too hard, so you won't see everything. Don't guess wildly: pick « Je ne sais pas » when you don't know, which tells the plan more than a lucky guess. It ends with one short writing task and one short speaking task. Expect 15 to 30 minutes.",
  "dontKnowOption": "Je ne sais pas",
  "priorKnowledge": {
    "source": "Duolingo French, section 2 unit 11 (learner-reported, 2026-10-24)",
    "notes": "About 300 words seen in Duolingo's word list: greetings, family, être/avoir/aller forms, s'appeler, venir de, j'habite, nationalities, food and café, clothes and colours, home, travel and transit, shopping and paying, frequency adverbs (souvent, parfois, toujours), parce que, vouloir, connaître, and a few passé composé forms (sommes perdus, j'ai). All standard European French. None of the Québec forms (allô as hi, bienvenue as you're welcome, fin de semaine, pas pire, chu, je reste à, dépanneur) appear.",
    "expectedPlacement": "Reading and vocabulary at Level 2–3; listening and speaking likely lower, around Level 1–2, because Duolingo trains these less. Québec-specific forms are new at every level.",
    "knownVocabIds": [
      "v01-bonjour", "v01-bonsoir", "v01-au-revoir", "v01-bonne-journee", "v01-bonne-soiree", "v01-merci", "v01-de-rien", "v01-svp", "v01-excusez-moi",
